Star battle?

--------------------------------------------------

For  $n \le 7$ , consider two adjacent columns. This is immediately impossible for  $n = 6$ , and for  $n = 7$  forces the dyed squares to be in the first, third, fifth, and last row, which gives a contradiction of every two adjacent rows.

For  $n = 4k, k \ge 2$ , we take the minimal star battle solution / IMO 2010 SL C3 solution and take a subset of stars. To get  $n = 4k+1, 4k+2, 4k+3$ , we may extend this grid and modify it slightly.

[asy]
int N = 5;
int m = 2*N;

for (int i = -m; i <= m; i += 1) {
draw((i,-m)--(i,m));
draw((-m,i)--(m,i));
}

// shape s = unitsquare;
for (int i = 0; i < N; i += 1) {
for (int j = 0; j < N; j += 1) {
pen p = green+white;
if ((i+j) == N || ((i+j) == N-1) || (i+j) == 0) {
p = purple+white;
}

filldraw(shift((2*i,2*j+1))*unitsquare, p);
filldraw(shift((-2*j-2,2*i))*unitsquare, p);
filldraw(shift((-2*i-1,-2*j-2))*unitsquare, p);
