If  $f(x) = x^3+px^2+qx+r=0$  has a root at  $x_{0}=1+\sqrt[3]{2}+\sqrt[3]{4}$ , then we obtain: $f(x_{0}) = (1+\sqrt[3]{2}+\sqrt[3]{4})^3 + p(1+\sqrt[3]{2}+\sqrt[3]{4})^2+q(1+\sqrt[3]{2}+\sqrt[3]{4})+r=0$ ;

or  $(3\sqrt[3]{4}+4\sqrt[3]{2}+5)p + (\sqrt[3]{4}+\sqrt[3]{2}+1)q + (12\sqrt[3]{4}+15\sqrt[3]{2}+19) + r=0$ .

or  $\begin{bmatrix} 3\sqrt[3]{4} && \sqrt[3]{4} && 0  4\sqrt[3]{2} && \sqrt[3]{2} && 0  5 && 1 && 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}p  q  r\end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix}-12\sqrt[3]{4}  -15\sqrt[3]{2}  -19\end{bmatrix} \Rightarrow \begin{bmatrix}p  q  r\end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix}-3  -3  -1\end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{Z}^{3}$ .

If  $f(x)=x^3-3x^2-3x-1$ , then  $f'(x) = 0 \Rightarrow 3x^2-6x-3 = 0 \Rightarrow x = \frac{6\pm\sqrt{36-4(3)(-3)}}{2(3)} \Rightarrow x = 1\pm \sqrt{2}$ ;

and checking  $f''(x)$  against these critical points yields:  $f''(x)=6x-6 \Rightarrow f(1+\sqrt{2}) = \sqrt{2} > 0$  (a minimum);  $f''(1-\sqrt{2}) = -\sqrt{2} < 0$  (a maximum).

We next observe that  $f_{MIN} = f(1+\sqrt{2}) = -2(3+2\sqrt{2})$  and  $f_{MAX} =f(1-\sqrt{2})= -2(3-2\sqrt{2}).$   Both local extrema are negative, and thus  $f$  has  $x_{0}=1+\sqrt[3]{2}+\sqrt[3]{4}$  as its only real root  $\Rightarrow \textcolor{red}{x^3-3x^2-3x-1}$  is our desired cubic with integer coefficients.
